    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "Prior to the EU-Turkey deal, the number of daily arrivals in the Aegean had peaked at over 5,000 people per day in early 2016 before the agreement was finalized.",
      "source_date": "2016-03-10"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "While 2015 saw over 1.8 million arrivals by sea [1], significant policy changes were implemented in early 2016 to drastically reduce these numbers. The deployment of the NATO fleet in February 2016 marked an intensified effort to intercept boats before they reached Greek waters [2]. Crucially, the EU-Turkey statement of March 18, 2016, established a mechanism to return migrants crossing illegally back to Turkey, effectively closing the primary route used during the previous peak [3]. Historical data shows that prior to this deal, daily crossings were extremely high, but the implementation of the deal led to an immediate and sharp decline in arrivals [4]. Given the timing of the deal (March) and the subsequent reduction in flow, it is highly probable that the total annual figure would fall well below the 1 million mark seen in 2015.",
