  "prediction": "no"
}

{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "On March 18, 2016, Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos and FARC senior negotiators confirmed that the agreed deadline of March 23 to sign a final peace accord could not be met.",
      "source_date": "2016-03-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "In the same announcement on March 18, 2016, the consensus from the negotiation table in Havana was that an agreement would be reached within the next month.",
      "source_date": "2016-03-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "As of March 18, 2016, there were still parts of the six-point agenda to be discussed and fine print to be ironed out before both parties could sign the accord.",
      "source_date": "2016-03-18"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "The peace negotiations between the Colombian government and FARC had been ongoing since 2012.",
      "source_date": "2012-08-29"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning: "
  "Based on the evidence, the parties explicitly stated on March 18, 2016, that they would not meet the March 23 deadline [1]. However, the same source indicates that the consensus among negotiators was that an agreement would be reached 'within the next month' [2]. Since the statement was made on March 18, 'within the next month" implies a target date sometime between March 18 and mid-to-late April 2016. Given that the deadline of May 1, 2016, is approximately one month after the March 18 statement, and the expressed consensus was to finish within that timeframe, it is highly probable the agreement would be signed before May 1, 2016. The remaining tasks (six-point agenda and fine print) were described as the only obstacles [3], and the timeline provided by the negotiators suggests they anticipated resolving these before the end of April."
  "probability_yes": 0.85,
  "prediction": "yes"
}

